WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.183 --> 00:00:07.736
In August of 1745, a sixteen-year-old girl prepared to marry a young man she did not love.

2
00:00:07.737 --> 00:00:13.463
The wedding was eagerly awaited by the whole Russian empire, but a somber mood haunted her. "As

3
00:00:13.464 --> 00:00:21.059
this day approached, I grew more deeply melancholic. My heart did not foresee great happiness," she wrote.

4
00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:27.842
Although her destiny had been decided for her, this remarkable woman made the best of the situation.

5
00:00:27.843 --> 00:00:36.280
Her intelligence and resolve led her to become the empress of Russia, whom the world would remember

6
00:00:36.281 --> 00:00:44.042
as Catherine the Great. She was born Sophia Augusta Frederica on May 2, 1729, in Stettin, Prussia. Her

7
00:00:44.043 --> 00:00:49.992
father was the prince of a small province, and her mother was related to Swedish royalty. Sophia’s

8
00:00:49.993 --> 00:00:55.521
ambitious parents were determined to marry their daughter to a powerful monarch. Private tutors educated Sophia, an

9
00:00:55.522 --> 00:01:01.027
eager student who enjoyed learning. In 1744 she traveled to Russia with her mother at the invitation

10
00:01:01.028 --> 00:01:07.654
of the country’s ruler. Russia’s Empress Elizabeth was searching for a wife for her nephew, Grand Duke

11
00:01:07.655 --> 00:01:16.123
Peter, the 15-year-old heir to the throne. Sophia’s charm and intelligence impressed the empress; she asked Sophia

12
00:01:16.124 --> 00:01:23.630
to stay in Russia and meet Peter. Sophia tried to befriend him but soon discovered that the

13
00:01:23.631 --> 00:01:35.221
grand duke had little interest in either marrying her or ruling the country&#8212;he preferred playing with toy

14
00:01:35.222 --> 00:01:41.323
soldiers. So she sat beside him for hours while he barked orders at his imaginary troops. Despite

15
00:01:41.324 --> 00:01:49.490
the indifference of Grand Duke Peter, the people and land ruled by Empress Elizabeth captivated the teenage

16
00:01:49.491 --> 00:01:55.321
Sophia. She devoted herself to learning about the fascinating country she hoped to call home. Tutors taught

17
00:01:55.322 --> 00:02:03.418
her Russian language and religion. She often studied late into the night, pacing the cold floor of

18
00:02:03.419 --> 00:02:13.151
her bedroom. The icy Russian winter took its toll; Sophia caught pleurisy, a dangerous inflammation of the lungs,

19
00:02:13.152 --> 00:02:19.698
and was confined to bed. She used the illness to her advantage by pretending to be asleep

20
00:02:19.699 --> 00:02:24.910
while her nurses chattered nearby. Listening to the women let Sophia peek inside the lives of ordinary

21
00:02:24.911 --> 00:02:29.295
Russians. She meant to understand them, for she hoped to win the country’s affection. She had already

22
00:02:29.296 --> 00:02:34.843
won the heart of its ruler&#8212;the empress worried terribly about Sophia’s health. Sophia further endeared herself to

23
00:02:34.844 --> 00:02:43.129
Elizabeth when she converted to the Russian Orthodox faith and took a new name, Catherine. The empress

24
00:02:43.130 --> 00:02:47.153
soon announced that Peter and Catherine would marry. Catherine sensed that she and Peter would not be

25
00:02:47.154 --> 00:02:53.981
happy together, but she turned her attention to a greater goal. She wrote, "At the bottom of

26
00:02:53.982 --> 00:03:05.034
my soul I had something . . . that never for a single moment let me doubt

27
00:03:05.035 --> 00:03:13.139
that . . . I would succeed in becoming the sovereign empress of Russia." The couple married

28
00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:21.474
on August 21, 1745, and Catherine's instincts about the marriage proved correct. The differences between them made

29
00:03:21.475 --> 00:03:28.132
their union an unhappy one. Despite being heir to the throne, Peter disliked Russia. He adored Prussia

30
00:03:28.133 --> 00:03:33.572
and its leader, Frederick II, who was Empress Elizabeth’s sworn enemy. But Catherine, resigned to a loveless marriage,

31
00:03:33.573 --> 00:03:45.552
was in love with Russia. She prided herself on having gained a deep understanding of its culture

32
00:03:45.553 --> 00:03:54.870
and people, and she prepared for what she felt was her true destiny: to rule the country

33
00:03:54.871 --> 00:04:00.170
in her own right. When Empress Elizabeth died in January of 1762, Peter became emperor and quickly

34
00:04:00.171 --> 00:04:06.113
formed an alliance with Frederick II. He even changed the uniforms of the Russian military to resemble

35
00:04:06.114 --> 00:04:18.156
the Prussians’. His actions angered the proud Russian military and turned public opinion against him. On July

36
00:04:18.157 --> 00:04:22.964
9, 1762, only a few months after Peter's coronation, military officers overthrew him and proclaimed Catherine empress

37
00:04:22.965 --> 00:04:31.029
of Russia. Peter died days later under mysterious circumstances. On September 22, 1762, Catherine was crowned Catherine

38
00:04:31.030 --> 00:04:34.974
II, empress of Russia. She intended to make Russia the greatest nation in Europe. But government corruption

39
00:04:34.975 --> 00:04:43.782
and lavish spending had nearly bankrupted the country; Catherine needed to know what resources the nation had.

40
00:04:43.783 --> 00:04:49.909
"How many towns are there in Russia?" she asked when she met with the senate. Nobody knew.

41
00:04:49.910 --> 00:04:55.445
She sent a clerk to buy a map and bring it to the senate so they could count

42
00:04:55.446 --> 00:05:05.583
the towns. Farms surrounded many of the towns, and Catherine believed that modernized farming of Russia’s vast

43
00:05:05.584 --> 00:05:12.655
acres would produce better, larger crops to export. She therefore sent advisers to England to learn new

44
00:05:12.656 --> 00:05:20.544
agricultural techniques and bring them back to Russia. Catherine also focused on international trade and expanded Russian

45
00:05:20.545 --> 00:05:24.370
exports in iron, timber, and textiles. She had replenished the treasury within three years. Russia had wealth;

46
00:05:24.371 --> 00:05:36.846
now it needed territory. Determined to avoid conflict with her European neighbors, Catherine turned her gaze to

47
00:05:36.847 --> 00:05:45.181
the Ottoman Empire in 1768. By attacking the Turks, she hoped to win access to seaports on

48
00:05:45.182 --> 00:05:50.181
the Black Sea, which were vital to increasing trade. The war raged on for six years and

49
00:05:50.182 --> 00:06:00.106
ended with a Russian victory. Under Catherine’s rule Russia also claimed part of Poland and absorbed Ukraine,

50
00:06:00.107 --> 00:06:09.658
increasing both its territory and population. Catherine eventually expanded Russia’s borders by over 200,000 square miles, making

51
00:06:09.659 --> 00:06:21.820
the country one of the largest in the world. As Russia grew in wealth and territory, Catherine

52
00:06:21.821 --> 00:06:27.048
worked to expand its cultural achievements, insisting that Russian culture could, and should, rival that of other

53
00:06:27.049 --> 00:06:33.469
European nations. She built Russia’s first public library and ordered schools built for children. She continued her own

54
00:06:33.470 --> 00:06:37.874
education through reading and corresponding with famous European philosophers. Intellectual and artistic ideas were flowing freely across

55
00:06:37.875 --> 00:06:44.750
Europe. Not to be outdone, Catherine relaxed censorship rules and encouraged the publishing of new Russian books.

56
00:06:44.751 --> 00:06:53.735
She also supported the arts, building a magnificent opera house and donating paintings and sculptures for young

57
00:06:53.736 --> 00:07:02.339
Russian artists to learn from. Her private collection became the Hermitage, the art museum that would eventually

58
00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:11.443
house the largest collection of paintings in the world. Catherine’s policies revealed her to be a more

59
00:07:11.444 --> 00:07:20.563
progressive leader than her predecessors, but she also clung to harsh practices that blemished her reign. Upon

60
00:07:20.564 --> 00:07:29.039
being crowned empress, she had vowed to free the serfs, lowly peasants who spent their lives as

61
00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:32.262
slave laborers on Russia's farms. Farming provided most of Russia’s income, however, and Catherine thought first of

62
00:07:32.263 --> 00:07:39.427
her treasury&#8212;the serfs remained in bondage. It was not the last promise Catherine broke. The French Revolution,

63
00:07:39.428 --> 00:07:45.419
erupting in the 1780s, saw ordinary citizens attack the aristocracy. Afraid her subjects might turn against her,

64
00:07:45.420 --> 00:07:52.125
Catherine acted quickly to protect her throne. She revived censorship and banished to Siberia writers who dared

65
00:07:52.126 --> 00:08:03.132
challenge her authority. Toward the end of her reign, she withdrew from the public eye, leaving the governance

66
00:08:03.133 --> 00:08:08.506
of Russia to advisers until her death on November 6, 1796. By taking control of her destiny,

67
00:08:08.507 --> 00:08:15.070
Catherine also took control of Russia’s. She is remembered as Catherine the Great for her role in

68
00:08:15.071 --> 00:08:20.621
shaping the future of her adopted country. She considered herself an enlightened ruler who advanced Russia’s standing

69
00:08:20.622 --> 00:08:29.647
in the world. Indeed, in just 35 years, its borders expanded, its wealth increased, and its cultural

70
00:08:29.648 --> 00:08:37.127
contributions soared. Catherine, betrothed to the heir to the Russian throne, became instead the bride of Russia.

